//? Title: k-Sparse Autoencoders: Hard Competition between Signed Activities
//? Subtitle: Stable ties, winner-only gradients, a linear 64-coordinate encoder and a matched ungated MNIST control
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A compact image representation can still distribute information across every code coordinate.
A k-sparse autoencoder limits how many coordinates are selected for each observation, forcing its
features to compete. This report derives hard signed-activity selection and its gradients, checks
ties and negative activities by hand, and trains the original 784–64–784 network on genuine MNIST.
Its linear encoder keeps the eight largest signed values during both training and inference.
With matched initial weights, minibatch order and optimization budget, the selected sparse model
has held-out MSE 0.05003, versus 0.04134 for the ungated control and 0.06780 for the fitting mean.
The sparse code therefore carries input information, but this short run gives no reconstruction
advantage over the matched plain model and establishes no classification or bit-rate claim.
)
\toc

> The method

For an image row \(x\in[0,1]^d\), the encoder computes linear activities
\(a=xW_e+b_e\in\mathbb R^F\). Let \(S_k(a)\) contain the indices of the largest
\(\min(k,F)\) **signed values**, with ties resolved by increasing feature index. The selector
\(m_j(a)=\mathbf1\{j\in S_k(a)\}\) is detached; the code and reconstruction are

$$
h=a\odot m(a),\qquad \hat x=\sigma(hW_d+b_d),\qquad
L=\frac1{Nd}\sum_{i,j}(\hat X_{ij}-X_{ij})^2.
$$
\alttext(The code multiplies linear activities by a detached binary selector of the largest signed values. An independent sigmoid decoder reconstructs the original image, scored by mean squared pixel error.)

The mask is recomputed from each observation at inference too. There is no ReLU in this encoder,
no sorting by absolute value, no L1 penalty and no straight-through gradient through the ranking.
Selected negative values remain negative. Exactly k slots are selected when k≤F, but a selected
zero does not become nonzero, so the number of nonzero code entries can be smaller. The model
retains F coordinate slots; sparsity does not alone specify a compressed storage format.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/k_sparse_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(56, 1100);
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Image x\n784 pixels")
      .node("a", {5, 0}, "Linear encoder\n64 signed activities")
      .node("m", {5, -2.5}, "Detached selector\nEight largest signed values")
      .node("h", {10, 0}, "Sparse code h = a × m\nEight selected slots")
      .node("r", {10, -3}, "Independent decoder\n784 sigmoid pixels")
      .node("l", {0, -6}, "Pixel MSE\nOriginal image is the target")
      .edge("x", "a")
      .edge("a", "m")
      .edge("a", "h")
      .edge("m", "h")
      .edge("h", "r")
      .edge("r", "l")
      .edge("x", "l")
      .save_svg("build/k_sparse_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/k_sparse_method.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The selector depends on encoder activities but is discrete. Reconstruction gradients pass through selected activities and both learned weight matrices.)
\alttext(A linear encoder branches into activities and a detached top-eight selector. Their product forms the sparse code, which feeds an independent sigmoid decoder and a clean reconstruction objective.)

Away from support changes, define
\(D=2(\hat X-X)\odot\hat X\odot(1-\hat X)/(Nd)\), and
\(E=(DW_d^\top)\odot M\). The continuous chain rule is

$$
\nabla_{W_d}L=H^\top D,\quad\nabla_{b_d}L=\sum_iD_i,\qquad
\nabla_{W_e}L=X^\top E,\quad\nabla_{b_e}L=\sum_iE_i.
$$
\alttext(Decoder gradients use the selected codes and sigmoid residuals. Encoder deltas multiply the decoder backpropagation by the binary selector before accumulating weight and bias gradients.)

Unselected encoder coordinates receive zero gradient for that observation. Decoder biases still
learn, and a feature can win on other observations. These are local fixed-support derivatives,
not derivatives of an everywhere smooth function. Selection by stable sorting costs
\(O(NF\log F)\), in addition to \(O(NdF)\) dense arithmetic. The implementation forms a dense
masked code and uses dense matrix multiplication; it does not claim sparse-kernel acceleration.

The reusable `models::CompetitiveMask` and `models::CompetitiveAutoencoder` now live in the core.
The same components support a separate lifetime mode for the later winner-take-all example:
that mode competes across observations per feature and uses ReLU with a training-only gate.
This report exclusively fits per-observation competition with a linear encoder. Its ungated
control has the same linear encoder, independent weights, dimensions and initialization.

> Four rows, stable ties and negative winners

For k=2, the four activity rows below exercise ordinary ordering, equal scores and an all-negative
row. Equal scores keep the lower index. In the last row, −1 and −2 win over −3 and −4 even though
the latter have larger magnitudes. Every mask entry and every derivative of the summed gated
activities is checked against a fixed hand-calculated value.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/k_sparse_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n  = dm::nn;
  const auto x = n::tensor(
                     {3., 1., 1., 0., 2., 4., 0., 0., -1., -2., 5., 5., -4., -1., -2., -3.},
                     {4, 4},
                     n::DType::Float64)
                     .requires_grad_();
  const auto mask     = n::models::CompetitiveMask(x, false, 2);
  const auto code     = x * mask;
  const auto gradient = n::grad(n::sum(code), {x})[0];
  const auto expected = n::tensor(
      {1., 1., 0., 0., 1., 1., 0., 0., 0., 0., 1., 1., 0., 1., 1., 0.}, {4, 4}, n::DType::Float64);
  if (mask.requires_grad() || n::max(n::abs(mask - expected)).item() != 0 ||
      n::max(n::abs(gradient - expected)).item() != 0)
    return 1;
  std::print(
      "| raw activities | selector | code | derivative of summed code |\n|---|---|---|---|\n");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(62, 1100);
  for (int row = 0; row < 4; ++row) {
    const auto raw  = n::select(x, 0, row).values_str();
    const auto kept = n::select(code, 0, row).values_str();
    std::print(
        "| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
        raw,
        n::select(mask, 0, row).values_str(),
        kept,
        n::select(gradient, 0, row).values_str());
    const auto from = "raw" + std::to_string(row), to = "code" + std::to_string(row);
    d.node(from, {0, -2.2 * row}, "Raw: " + raw)
        .node(to, {8, -2.2 * row}, "Code: " + kept)
        .edge(from, to);
  }
  d.save_svg("build/k_sparse_toy.svg");
}
```
