\caption(Signed top-two selection, including a lower-index tie and an all-negative row. Minus-zero entries are filtered values, not retained negative activities.)
\alttext(Four paired rows show raw activity vectors and their masked codes. The last retains minus one and minus two, replacing minus four and minus three with zero.)

> Checking the limits of hard signed selection

This signed selector is not the Euclidean best k-term projection, which would retain the largest
magnitudes. For \(a=(-4,-1,-2,-3)\), signed top-two gives \((0,-1,-2,0)\), with squared discarded
error 25; retaining magnitudes 4 and 3 would give error 5. It is not generally idempotent either:
applying signed top-two again selects the two newly introduced zeros, giving the all-zero vector.
The network applies the gate once per forward pass; it does not repeatedly prune its own code.

At a selection boundary, the code can jump. With k=1, activities \((1-\delta,1)\) and
\((1+\delta,1)\) are only \(2\delta\) apart, but their codes approach \((0,1)\) and \((1,0)\).
The output separation approaches \(\sqrt2\), even as the input separation tends to zero.
Consequently neither continuity at ties nor a global nonexpansive-projection bound follows.
The tie rule makes the result deterministic, not differentiable there. The program checks this
counterexample and finite differences through a complete small model strictly away from ties.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/k_sparse_support.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n          = dm::nn;
  const auto type      = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto x         = n::tensor({-4., -1., -2., -3.}, {1, 4}, type);
  const auto once      = x * n::models::CompetitiveMask(x, false, 2);
  const auto twice     = once * n::models::CompetitiveMask(once, false, 2);
  const auto magnitude = n::tensor({-4., 0., 0., -3.}, {1, 4}, type);
  if (n::sum(n::square(x - once)).item() != 25 || n::sum(n::square(x - magnitude)).item() != 5 ||
      n::max(n::abs(twice)).item() != 0)
    return 1;
  std::print(
      "Signed discarded squared error: 25; magnitude projection error: 5.\n\n| delta | input "
      "separation | code separation |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (double delta : {.1, .01, .0001, .000001}) {
    const auto left  = n::tensor({1 - delta, 1.}, {1, 2}, type),
               right = n::tensor({1 + delta, 1.}, {1, 2}, type);
    const auto a     = left * n::models::CompetitiveMask(left, false, 1),
               b     = right * n::models::CompetitiveMask(right, false, 1);
    std::print(
        "| {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.9f} |\n",
        delta,
        n::sqrt(n::sum(n::square(left - right))).item(),
        n::sqrt(n::sum(n::square(a - b))).item());
  }
  n::ParameterStore p(1931, type);
  const auto        input = n::tensor({.1, .2, .8, .7, .3, .4}, {2, 3}, type).requires_grad_();
  n::models::CompetitiveAutoencoder(p, input, false, false, 3, 4, 2);
  const n::TensorFunction objective = [&](const auto& a) {
    return n::mse(
        n::models::CompetitiveAutoencoder(p, a[0], false, false, 3, 4, 2).training_reconstruction,
        a[0]);
  };
  if (!n::gradcheck(objective, {input}, {.atol = 1e-8, .rtol = 1e-5}).ok ||
      !n::gradgradcheck(objective, {input}, {.atol = 1e-8, .rtol = 1e-5}).ok)
    return 1;
  std::print("\nComplete-model input first/second finite differences pass on fixed support.\n");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(58, 1100);
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Original\n[-4, -1, -2, -3]")
      .node("once", {5, 0}, "One signed top-two gate\n[0, -1, -2, 0]")
      .node("twice", {10, 0}, "A second gate\n[0, 0, 0, 0]")
      .edge("x", "once")
      .edge("once", "twice")
      .text({5, -2}, "Inserted zeros outrank negative retained activities on the second pass")
      .save_svg("build/k_sparse_support.svg");
}
```
