\caption(Seven matched rows compare reconstruction, constant and code-ablation controls. Integer pixel enlargement is three, with two-pixel gaps.)
\alttext(A seven-row contact sheet compares original digits, sparse and plain reconstructions, the fitting mean, sparse errors and two misinformative-code controls.)

The optional development oracle `scripts/check_k_sparse_autoencoder.py` reads the native
archives without preparing data or fitting a model. Its independent Float64 NumPy calculation
checks all 202,400 first-batch parameter derivatives (maximum absolute difference below 1.7e-10),
both Float32 first Adam updates bit for bit, every retained gate probe and all 2,822,400 predicted
pixels across the three pools and code controls. Pool predictions agree within 5.1e-7, with no
changed rank decisions; the smallest selected-versus-unselected score gap is 6.5e-5. All source
arrays in 27 report archives also match an execution of the original C++ example, including
initial outputs, twelve parameter snapshots and the eight-row final minibatch probes.

> Conclusion

Hard competition creates winner-only gradients on each locally fixed support, while its signed
ranking and tie behaviour impose limits that a smooth or magnitude-based projection would not
have. The original small model and explicit optimization loop remain intact, with matched weights,
row order, validation selection and deployment checks. Its code carries sample information, but
this three-epoch study favours the ungated control on pixel MSE. It does not establish a semantic
representation, a classification improvement, sparse-kernel speedup or a file-compression bit rate.

> References

- Makhzani, A. and Frey, B. (2013, revised 2014). [k-Sparse Autoencoders](https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.5663).
  This educational variant preserves signed linear activities and hard per-image selection but uses
  raw unit pixels, an untied sigmoid decoder and a small reconstruction experiment.
- Makhzani, A. and Frey, B. (2014, revised 2015). [Winner-Take-All Autoencoders](https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.2752).
  The reusable component also supports a separate lifetime mode; no lifetime model is fitted here.
- LeCun, Y., Bottou, L., Bengio, Y. and Haffner, P. (1998). Gradient-based learning applied to document recognition.
  [Proceedings of the IEEE 86(11), 2278–2324](https://doi.org/10.1109/5.726791).
